00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.270
AMY BANZAERT: I hope you're
getting excited about the trip.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.980
So there are a few things that I
managed to skip over last time.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.150
One is solar cookers.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:38.840
It somehow got dropped
from my presentation

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.940
and they're a kind of
key part of cooking

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.370
that I want to talk about.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.870
But at least most of you guys
have investigated a little bit

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:45.630
on your own.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.140
And then we're also just talking
about the energy to boil water.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:54.590
So in terms of solar cookers,
as Grupo Phoenix mentioned,

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.700
there are three types.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.730
So there's the concentrator
or parabolic method,

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.860
where you have some sort
of parabolic collector

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.910
that's aiming the heat onto your
cooking cook stove, which is

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.800
then heating up the material.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:15.080
You have the box method, which
is using a greenhouse method,

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.350
where you have a reflective
device reflecting

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:19.370
into the greenhouse.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:23.390
And that's pretty much the
design that Grupo Phoenix uses.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:26.060
I wouldn't call it a
hybrid, because the hybrid

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.370
has both that
greenhouse, but also uses

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.730
some parabolic features
to collect the energy.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.050
And you can see that the cost--

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:39.710
you can imagine the cost of
these three devices shown

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:40.470
varies.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.740
And so you can get very
inexpensive versions

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:43.528
of all of these--

00:01:43.528 --> 00:01:45.320
but the parabolic is
definitely the hardest

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.960
to get super cheap--

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.580
and very expensive versions.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.480
So I wanted to start
by just talking

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.850
about what you think
might be some pros

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.785
and cons of solar cookers.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.289
AUDIENCE: Can I
ask you a question?

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:14.100
Is the material that they use--

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.386
what is it?

00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:23.690
[INAUDIBLE] just sheet
metal or aluminum foil.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:25.580
AMY BANZAERT: So sheet
metal is pretty--

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:33.540
yeah, it's hard to get a really
good reflection off of it.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.440
So I think I've
seen some that are

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:40.970
basically mirrors, so that's,
I think, what that is.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:47.420
This is a plastic that's
super shiny, that's

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.010
just laminated
onto the cardboard,

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:53.252
so a whole range from
expensive to non.

00:02:53.252 --> 00:02:55.988
AUDIENCE: I have a question.

00:02:55.988 --> 00:03:00.560
Is the concentrator-- what is
it concentrating it toward,

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.210
the box or toward--

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:04.460
AMY BANZAERT: No, so
this is an open pan.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:09.050
So it's just concentrating
it on the pan.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:13.760
So this is the difference--
the concentrator is just

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.882
shining a strong ray
of heat onto something.

00:03:17.882 --> 00:03:20.480
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.810
AMY BANZAERT: Solar cookers
are very rarely electrical.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:24.890
I've never seen--

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:31.100
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] was having
a meter panel [INAUDIBLE] house

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.330
where you could
charge your cellphone

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.398
and you could also cook.

00:03:36.398 --> 00:03:38.190
But then that would
have definitely-- we're

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.740
thinking about the panel
outside on the roof

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:45.420
collecting the rays,
collecting the solar energy,

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.870
transferring it into
electrical energy,

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:52.932
and getting it to solar
cooker [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:54.390
AMY BANZAERT: So
I think that would

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:59.010
be a really good estimation
exercise, to figure out

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.550
what it takes to make
a solar cooker that's

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.710
an electrical solar cooker.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.880
Because do you remember how many
Watts a typical stove or oven

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:09.910
is?

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:10.890
AUDIENCE: A lot.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:11.682
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:20.000
So a typical like burner
is 1,000 to 1,500 Watts.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.220
A typical oven is 3,000 Watts.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.670
So we're talking
really different scopes

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:27.740
in terms of what a
cellphone charger is

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.960
and what cooking
is, needs to be.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.580
And so that's a high
barrier to entry.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.810
So you need to look into
the viability of that.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.990
OK, so can we make
a list of what

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:47.252
you think are the
bonuses of solar cookers,

00:04:47.252 --> 00:04:48.710
and what are some
of the drawbacks?

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:49.210
Benjy.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.620
AUDIENCE: It's free
after you buy it.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.695
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
so it's free energy.

00:04:57.878 --> 00:04:58.920
AUDIENCE: Zero emissions.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:04:59.712
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:05:03.998 --> 00:05:05.540
AUDIENCE: A con is
that you might not

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:07.207
be able to have right
outside your home,

00:05:07.207 --> 00:05:10.580
like that girl standing in a
river, which is probably not

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:12.750
outside her home.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:18.437
AMY BANZAERT: So it
needs to be outside.

00:05:18.437 --> 00:05:20.270
AUDIENCE: You have to
find a sunny location.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:21.062
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.155
AUDIENCE: So it's [INAUDIBLE]
weather dependent.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:44.623
AMY BANZAERT: Seth.

00:05:44.623 --> 00:05:46.540
AUDIENCE: Apart from the
concentrator cookers,

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.100
they can't fry food.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.030
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so your
cooking options are limited.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:04.150
So yeah, you can absolutely
fry food with the concentrator,

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.280
but it can be hard
to get a low heat,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.800
if you want a low heat
in a concentrator.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.500
Carter.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:12.640
AUDIENCE: Portability.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:16.405
AMY BANZAERT: Portability, yeah,
so I'll put that in the middle.

00:06:19.237 --> 00:06:20.320
Well, and there's a real--

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.112
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
actually, portability

00:06:22.112 --> 00:06:28.150
would be grow the
economy of scale.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:29.360
Does that make sense?

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:32.932
You can move it around,
but only to a certain size.

00:06:32.932 --> 00:06:34.640
AMY BANZAERT: Well,
but you can see here,

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.830
like this-- this is a highly
portable solar cooker.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:38.540
That parabolic
generator actually

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:41.900
folds up into something
that's not much bigger--

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.810
it's significantly
smaller than this.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.640
So that is super portable.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:51.090
Whereas that concentrator
is a fixed location.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:54.230
So some are portable,
some are not.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:56.900
And then if they're portable,
that can be helpful,

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:58.250
but then it's limited by size.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.769
So I think actually leaving it
in the middle is reasonable.

00:07:01.769 --> 00:07:06.996
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
scale or size [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:06.996 --> 00:07:09.528
you're limited to certain
sizes [INAUDIBLE]..

00:07:09.528 --> 00:07:10.320
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:18.345
I don't if that is any more
true than it is for any stove.

00:07:24.335 --> 00:07:25.043
AUDIENCE: Touche.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:28.815
AMY BANZAERT: Any
other thoughts?

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:35.250
Yeah, Missy.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.375
AUDIENCE: Well, obviously,
a lot of your emissions,

00:07:37.375 --> 00:07:40.600
you have the benefit
of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.600
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
so no emissions,

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:47.080
which yields better health.

00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:58.260
AUDIENCE: There's [INAUDIBLE]
no preparation time.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:02.450
You don't have to
collect the fuel.

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:07.070
AMY BANZAERT: No
fuel collection,

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:10.160
which sort of links to that
one, because it's either

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:11.780
time or money, generally.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:12.584
Sid.

00:08:12.584 --> 00:08:15.040
AUDIENCE: I guess a con would
be that if you're constantly

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.930
changing the angle, and you're
cooking for a long period

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:19.344
of time, [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.946
AMY BANZAERT: OK, yeah, Benjy.

00:08:31.946 --> 00:08:33.630
AUDIENCE: For fires [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:44.780
So if someone were
to have [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:48.050
I mean, you're not using any
energy anyway so [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:50.270
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
because people

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:56.180
do use their fires for
heating, for mosquito control,

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.960
for light, sometimes.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:08:58.460
Yeah.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:04.870
OK, super, so I think
that's a pretty good list.

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:07.870
To give you some
background, solar cookers

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:10.900
were invented in the mid 1700s.

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:13.440
So they've been around
for a really long time.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:18.160
And I think that is
an indicator of how

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:21.400
this is a great
technology in some cases.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:25.030
But isn't ever going to be
the killer app that solves

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.580
the world's cooking challenges.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:29.860
Because one would think
if they've been around

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:32.590
since the 1700s
that the refinement

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:33.970
that takes it to
killer app would

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:35.920
have been developed already.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:37.570
But the cons of it--

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:39.910
weather dependent,
it's time dependent,

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:41.460
you need to be in
a sunny location,

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:42.460
you need to be outside--

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.990
are all really huge.

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:48.460
So some of the challenges
with the concentrators

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:51.340
are the glare and
just how hot it is.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:54.910
So it's kind of hard to
see because the quality

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:58.060
of the picture has gone down
going through the projector.

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:00.610
But this woman is
grimacing because it's

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:04.840
so bright that it's
actually hard to deal with,

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:09.130
and it's also very
hot where it is.

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:09.867
Yeah.

00:10:09.867 --> 00:10:10.700
AUDIENCE: Dangerous.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:11.650
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:14.863
So the concentrators in
particular are dangerous.

00:10:14.863 --> 00:10:16.780
And so people are really
concerned, especially

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:20.980
with kids, getting
not permanently,

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:23.480
but temporary blinded or burned.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:26.930
There's also issues that you can
imagine how easy that would be.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:27.430
Hola.

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:34.120
TA: So I have a really quick
question for all of you.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.990
Anyone wearing shoes with laces?

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:39.190
Can you please stand up?

00:10:46.737 --> 00:10:49.070
And the rest of you just look
around and get a good look

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:55.031
at who has laces
and who doesn't.

00:10:55.031 --> 00:10:56.810
Get this one clipped on here.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:01.940
OK, thank you, guys.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:02.648
You can sit down.

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:10.160
So I hate to break
it to you like this,

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:13.130
but I can see a lot of the
way you tied your laces.

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:17.510
And a lot of you just use the
simple bunny ears kind of knot

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:21.230
you learned when you were five
or six, which is great when

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:24.600
you learned it and worked well.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:28.607
But honestly now, it's
kind of a dumb not to use.

00:11:28.607 --> 00:11:30.440
I know you've been using
it your whole life,

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:33.920
but I can stand up
here and tell you

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.520
that because I'm a graduate
student here in Mechanical

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:39.920
Engineering, which
if you haven't heard

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:42.560
is probably the best
Mechanical Engineering

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:45.120
Department in the world.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.090
So you know I've learned
all about design,

00:11:47.090 --> 00:11:50.420
and good designs
and bad designs.

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:56.273
And these bunny ear knots
are pretty bad designs.

00:11:56.273 --> 00:11:58.190
And if you want, we could
sit here and analyze

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:01.290
the stresses in them, and where
are the equilibrium points,

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:04.550
and why does it stay tied, and
why does it always come untied.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:07.520
But the summary of all of
that is just it's a bad knot.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.310
And you guys should
learn a new one.

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:13.430
But thankfully,
I'm here, and I'm

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:16.130
going to go through and teach
you how to tie a better knot.

00:12:20.995 --> 00:12:23.120
So I'm going to kind of
step out of that character,

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:25.940
and ask you just
real quickly to share

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:30.322
thoughts or feelings of that
experience, and what I said

00:12:30.322 --> 00:12:32.030
or what I did that
made you think or feel

00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:33.422
the way you are now.

00:12:33.422 --> 00:12:35.390
AUDIENCE: I don't actually care.

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:39.020
TA: OK, that works.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:41.090
Other, anyone else?

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:43.780
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] I
don't have my shoes tied.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:50.360
AUDIENCE: You pointed out the
people who are doing it wrong,

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:51.950
which might make
them feel bad, but I

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:54.200
don't think I actually feel
too bad about the way they

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:55.060
tie their shoes.

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:01.053
TA: Good.

00:13:01.053 --> 00:13:02.220
AUDIENCE: How have you been?

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:03.270
TA: Doing well.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:11.050
So any other thoughts
about how I came across?

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:12.660
I obviously have
this idea that I

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:16.080
want to get you to learn how
to tie your shoes differently.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.925
Do you think that was
the best approach?

00:13:18.925 --> 00:13:21.300
AUDIENCE: So it was good
because you're really confident,

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:23.670
and so it was easy
to listen to you.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:26.730
And your argument in the
flow of your conversation

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:28.740
was really easy to follow.

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:31.355
But I also think you
were overconfident,

00:13:31.355 --> 00:13:33.160
because it was a simple topic.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:36.478
And in fact, as you [? write, ?]
the MIT thing, I think for us,

00:13:36.478 --> 00:13:38.436
it's good, because we're
all at MIT, of course.

00:13:38.436 --> 00:13:41.707
But I don't know how it would
go over with other people.

00:13:41.707 --> 00:13:43.290
So you think-- I
don't know, it seemed

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:45.120
like you thought you
were better than us.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:46.080
TA: Like arrogant.

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:47.170
AUDIENCE: Yeah, exactly.

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:47.670
TA: Yeah.

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:50.790
I was trying to be.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:51.300
Right.

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:54.360
I mean, I also assumed
there's a problem.

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:56.970
I assume that you're not
happy with the way you're

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:01.130
tying your shoes, and there's
just this other, better way.

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:03.720
I mean, like you said,
I ostracized someone who

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.880
tied their shoes differently.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:08.580
I didn't introduce
myself at all.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:10.510
I just kind of barged
in here, and said, hey,

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:12.030
let me tell you about this.

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:14.580
Just the personal connection is
a pretty important thing when

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:15.705
you're working with people.

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:20.580
Also, I didn't really ask
any information from you

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:22.680
other than, could
you stand up if you

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:25.110
have shoes that tie with laces.

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:27.270
There was no other
exchange of information.

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:28.770
And so if I'm coming
in here and I'm

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:31.800
trying to help you with
what I assume is a problem,

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:34.050
it's good to interact and
get some kind of information

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:36.690
from you guys.

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:40.380
And also, I kind
of imposed my value

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:45.450
of keeping shoes tied
for a long time over what

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:47.700
might be a value of
ease to tie your shoe

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:50.070
or ease to untie your
shoe, or just familiarity,

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:51.390
or the way the knot looks.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:52.890
You like the way
it looks, so you're

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:55.050
going to tie the bunny
ears over some other knot.

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:56.873
So I kind of just
assumed all these things,

00:14:56.873 --> 00:14:58.540
and just put them on
you, and said, hey,

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:01.900
here's a better way of doing it.

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:04.590
So I wanted to use it
as a simple illustration

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:07.530
of some circumstances
you might find yourself

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.660
in going to Nicaragua
soon, when you're

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:12.960
working with people
there on the projects

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:16.530
that you've been working
on so far, where you're

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:19.560
going into an entirely
different culture,

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:22.500
working in communities
where you really don't

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:24.600
know how everything works.

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:26.400
And it'd be really
easy to come across

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.235
in these same kinds of ways.

00:15:29.235 --> 00:15:31.110
So I wanted to just take
a minute, if we can,

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:34.080
to divide up into the teams--

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:36.630
you guys have been
working in teams--

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:41.615
and just talk about how you
might approach a situation

00:15:41.615 --> 00:15:42.990
where you show up
to a community,

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:45.780
you have a community
partner, and you

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:49.050
have the project that you've
been working on all term.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:54.600
And how would you go about
approaching the situation where

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:55.410
you just show up?

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:58.020
You get out of the van, or you
get off the back of the truck,

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:00.605
and you show up--

00:16:00.605 --> 00:16:01.230
what do you do?

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:04.800
How do you approach the
leaders that you interact with,

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:08.020
or just the people there?

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:11.153
And so I remember last year,
we had about four teams or so.

00:16:11.153 --> 00:16:12.570
AUDIENCE: This
year there's three.

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:13.528
TA: OK, so three teams.

00:16:13.528 --> 00:16:15.330
That actually works perfectly.

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:16.858
And right before
we do this, I'll

00:16:16.858 --> 00:16:18.150
introduce myself a little more.

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:18.900
I'm Steve Ray.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:20.098
I know of some of you.

00:16:20.098 --> 00:16:22.140
I'm a PhD student in
Mechanical Engineering here,

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:25.800
and helped out with
D-Lab: Energy last year.

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:27.453
But in order to
graduate, I needed

00:16:27.453 --> 00:16:29.370
to spend a little more
time on my dissertation

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:32.940
this year, so I couldn't
help out more than this.

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:34.830
But that's it.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.720
I went to Nicaragua last
year and it was a great time.

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:39.090
So you guys should be pumped.

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:40.932
You leave Friday or Saturday?

00:16:40.932 --> 00:16:41.640
AUDIENCE: Sunday.

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:44.580
TA: Sunday, OK, cool.

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:47.140
So let's just break up and then
we can quickly talk about it.

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:51.390
And the three of us will just
help facilitate and help you

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:53.880
guys think about how
you could approach

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.050
this in a positive way.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:57.550
AMY BANZAERT: I
just want to mention

00:16:57.550 --> 00:17:01.050
that the shoe tying, I think,
is very comparable to cooking.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:05.670
So you guys have been tying your
shoes since kindergarten-ish.

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:07.650
And most people have
been in the kitchen

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:13.109
since before they could walk,
and cooking from seven or eight

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:13.990
years old.

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:16.680
And so coming in and saying
you're cooking wrong,

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:19.079
which is somewhat what
some of us are saying--

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:22.109
hopefully not the [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:22.109 --> 00:17:24.390
But fundamentally
suggesting changes

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:27.480
is comparable that
it sounds ludicrous

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:30.340
and what are you talking
about on the same level.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:33.830
TA: And sometimes,
if there's something

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:36.620
that's relatively new,
you might not even

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:39.680
be aware of the problems with it
because you've just been doing

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:42.330
it the same way the whole time.

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:44.390
So if you observe
something and you

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:46.020
ask them, do you any
problems with it,

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:48.020
so things you can improve,
they say, not really.

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:49.200
It works pretty well.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.430
And if you see things that
you think could be improved,

00:17:52.430 --> 00:17:55.520
you could ask
them, well, what do

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:59.240
you think about decreasing
the time for this to work,

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:01.040
by making this change?

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:05.690
Or something like that, where it
might not be totally apparent,

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:09.860
but you can still ask
them how they would do it.

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:12.155
But starting with
what they see is huge.

00:18:14.887 --> 00:18:16.220
AMY BANZAERT: And then I think--

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:17.120
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:17.810
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.977
AMY BANZAERT: --really proud
of their solar cookers.

00:18:19.977 --> 00:18:26.150
[INAUDIBLE] and so
treading carefully there,

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:29.420
respecting what they've
accomplished while providing

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:34.410
positive ideas for changes.

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:35.060
Thanks.

00:18:35.060 --> 00:18:37.393
All right, so I know that was
kind of bizarre and weird,

00:18:37.393 --> 00:18:39.600
but there's something
like communities,

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:42.560
just walking in while they're
going about their daily lives,

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:44.840
and getting
completely interrupted

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:46.830
so that you can talk to them.

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:49.970
And so just thinking about how
awkward and weird that felt,

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:52.400
and therefore, what
you can do that

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:54.650
wasn't like what Steve did--
because he was definitely

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:57.350
doing worst-case scenario--
in order to minimize

00:18:57.350 --> 00:19:00.200
that awkwardness and weirdness.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:01.340
So back to solar cookers--

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:08.960
so we were talking
about the concentrator.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:11.310
It can be so hot
as to be dangerous,

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:14.610
especially for kids,
but really for everyone.

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:17.690
You can see that is
a big wind catcher,

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:19.640
so they can blow
over pretty easily.

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:22.680
That's actually an issue
with the Grupo Phoenix

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:25.910
solar cookers, too, because
this panel is so big

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:28.160
that it catches in the wind.

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:32.240
And then there's also a risk of
when you're stirring, spilling

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:35.390
food onto the reflector, and
then damaging the reflector

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:36.600
and how well it works.

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:38.900
So there's some real
drawbacks from the parabolic.

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:42.210
From the greenhouse,
it's a lower temperature.

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:45.530
And so you can really
only cook certain things.

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:47.690
But there's no
burning happening,

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:50.480
and you really don't need to
pay much attention to it, apart

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:52.850
from adjusting the
angle to the sun.

00:19:55.490 --> 00:19:58.280
And then the combo
has some of the pros

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:02.250
and some of the
cons of the others.

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:06.200
So we talked about the
benefits are pretty--

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:10.160
oh, and the other thing here
is you really can't stir this.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:12.360
Because as soon
as you open it up,

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:14.000
you're losing all
your heat, and you

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:15.330
have to start all over again.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:17.690
So it's a challenging device.

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:20.910
AUDIENCE: What's
the box made out of?

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:23.550
AMY BANZAERT: So boxes are
made out of almost anything.

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:25.920
I've seen plastic, I've
seen wood, I've seen metal.

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:32.280
So some of the challenges that
people can be frustrated about

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:35.440
in addition-- so one is that
you have to cook outdoors.

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:39.030
And so in cultures where
cooking indoors is standard,

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:41.740
cooking outdoors poses
a lot of challenges.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:43.930
So one is just the
need for privacy.

00:20:43.930 --> 00:20:47.610
Another is just risks of theft,
of either your food and/or

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:49.260
your cooker.

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:52.260
And it's also,
for people who are

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:55.890
used to cooking around a fire,
it's a little less social,

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:56.710
or can be.

00:20:56.710 --> 00:21:01.170
And so that can be a
flip side of frustration.

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:03.630
The super affordable
ones, like this one

00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:05.160
is made out of cardboard.

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:07.470
And so they don't last very
long because termites tend

00:21:07.470 --> 00:21:09.960
to eat them or other insects.

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:13.170
But they're very inexpensive.

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:15.900
And then they use
a plastic bag just

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:17.550
as your cover for
your greenhouse--

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:20.413
and again, very inexpensive,
but pretty short life.

00:21:20.413 --> 00:21:22.830
And so then you have, with all
these things breaking down,

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:24.120
you have a supply chain issue.

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:25.495
You need to make
sure that people

00:21:25.495 --> 00:21:29.940
are able to get replacements
and able to afford replacements.

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.400
The size, oftentimes--

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:35.160
I gave you a hard time,
Carter, about the size thing.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.470
But it's true that
actually, a lot of the times

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:39.870
they're relatively small
for how much they can cook.

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:42.390
And so you can't always cook
for all of your family members.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:43.500
That's frustrating.

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:45.900
There was an
interesting case study

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:49.920
where solar cookers
were brought to Kakuma,

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:54.450
which is a Kenyan refugee camp
for refugees from the Sudan.

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:57.330
And the Solar
Cookers International

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:00.720
gave away a bunch of cookers,
and there was actually

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:02.710
this cooker right here.

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:07.050
And so people are getting
their solar cookers for free.

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:10.163
And cooking was a huge
issue in these refugee camps

00:22:10.163 --> 00:22:12.330
because women were having
to leave the camp in order

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:13.380
to get firewood.

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:15.300
And when they were, they
were regularly raped.

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:18.840
And so they needed
an alternative

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:23.610
pretty severely because there
was no safe option for cooking.

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:27.570
And they also were spending
over 50% of their income

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:30.280
on firewood, if they
were buying it--

00:22:30.280 --> 00:22:31.770
so really outrageous.

00:22:31.770 --> 00:22:36.630
And in the end, what they found
is that less than 20% of people

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:39.540
ended up using the solar
cookers more than one or two

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:40.690
days per week.

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:44.430
So that's a pretty
low rate of usage

00:22:44.430 --> 00:22:47.250
for something when you're
facing really severe costs,

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:51.840
either to your bodily well-being
or your income, that severe.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:55.740
So I think that speaks to the
challenges of solar cooker

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:58.705
adoption-- that people
don't love to use them.

00:22:58.705 --> 00:23:01.080
AUDIENCE: Was that just because
they were not used to it,

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:03.420
or were there any major
kind of design flaws

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:04.650
that made them useless?

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:06.858
AMY BANZAERT: Well, so people
really don't like them.

00:23:06.858 --> 00:23:10.200
These cookers work, but
all of these real issues.

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:12.442
Like you can only cook
for lunch, basically.

00:23:12.442 --> 00:23:13.650
You can't cook for breakfast.

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:15.060
You can't cook for dinner.

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:17.650
You can only cook certain
types of foods, et cetera,

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:21.220
et cetera-- make them a
really of limited value.

00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:24.870
So on the flip side at
Kakuma is that there

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:27.240
were a bunch of single
men there who were

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:28.530
separated from their families.

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:36.540
So normally in the Sudan,
if you're a single guy,

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:37.650
your mom cooks for you.

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:40.570
And it's not normal
for men to cook at all.

00:23:40.570 --> 00:23:42.880
So either your wife or your
mom is cooking for you.

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:45.673
And so these single men were
separated from their mom,

00:23:45.673 --> 00:23:47.340
so they didn't have
anyone to cook with.

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:48.990
And it was really
socially inappropriate

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:50.130
for them to cook with wood.

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:52.020
And so they were
actually the adopters who

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:54.600
use the solar cookers because
it was appropriate for them

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:56.490
to use these, because
they're really not

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:59.703
considered stoves the
way a firewood stove

00:23:59.703 --> 00:24:00.870
would be considered a stove.

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:02.430
So that was the
success story, was

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:05.640
that when you needed a
radically different option,

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:07.785
it was relatively well adopted.

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:16.440
And the other aspect
is that in this camp,

00:24:16.440 --> 00:24:19.410
there was a food scarcity,
also, which is unsurprising.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:23.190
And so asking
people to experiment

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:24.855
with how they cook
when they don't

00:24:24.855 --> 00:24:29.353
know if they have enough food
is a huge request of people.

00:24:29.353 --> 00:24:31.020
And so I think that's
part of the reason

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:33.360
for the lack of adoption
was that I'm not

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:36.345
willing to try this wacky stove
because what if I ruin my food?

00:24:36.345 --> 00:24:38.220
That means I don't eat
today and I don't know

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:41.310
when I'll be eating very much.

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:45.570
So that was the summary
of solar cookers.

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:48.880
There are any questions
on solar cookers?

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:51.650
I'm sorry, I dropped that over
the charcoal lecture or stove

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:52.150
lecture.

00:24:52.150 --> 00:24:53.840
That would have been
the time to do it.

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:55.690
I don't know how it got lost.

00:24:55.690 --> 00:25:00.550
OK, so now we're finally going
to talk about the trip a bit,

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:03.257
reverting back to what
Steve was talking about.

00:25:03.257 --> 00:25:05.590
This lecture's a little
bouncing around intentionally so

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:08.380
that Steve could interrupt us.

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:11.450
So the trip goals
are, number one,

00:25:11.450 --> 00:25:14.500
to have some well-identified,
well-understood,

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:17.800
and well-documented
projects, ideally highly

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:19.280
relevant to D-Lab: Energy.

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:23.500
However, if you find some that
are not at all energy projects,

00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:25.603
but seem like
interesting projects,

00:25:25.603 --> 00:25:27.520
we can often take these
projects and feed them

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:29.720
into D-Lab Design or
other D-Lab classes.

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:31.285
So if you learn
about some others,

00:25:31.285 --> 00:25:32.410
feel free to document them.

00:25:32.410 --> 00:25:35.020
It's really helpful to
D-Lab as a community

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:37.810
to have those extra projects.

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:40.660
And then we've talked about
a few projects for you guys.

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:42.963
There may be others that
come up that are either ideal

00:25:42.963 --> 00:25:44.380
for you to work
on this semester--

00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:49.510
and that's fine to change
course like that-- and/or

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:52.670
for future generations of D-Lab
energy students to work on.

00:25:52.670 --> 00:25:55.870
So please document any
projects you come across.

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:57.700
And I will be getting
you, on Friday,

00:25:57.700 --> 00:25:59.830
the sheet of how to
document so that you

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:02.410
have a good sense of what
information you need to gather.

00:26:02.410 --> 00:26:05.032
But hopefully, we're
also getting that today.

00:26:05.032 --> 00:26:07.240
Second is to really understand
the communities you're

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:09.640
working in so you can do
a good job of designing

00:26:09.640 --> 00:26:11.290
with and for them.

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:12.380
What are their needs?

00:26:12.380 --> 00:26:13.570
What are their finances?

00:26:13.570 --> 00:26:17.530
What is their health, their
education, culture, government?

00:26:17.530 --> 00:26:21.072
Both less critically, though
some somewhat critically in

00:26:21.072 --> 00:26:22.780
terms of understanding
when grids arrive,

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:25.072
the formal government, also
like the informal community

00:26:25.072 --> 00:26:27.880
government that
exists, and along

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:32.260
those lines, formal, informal
leaders, lifestyles, work, et

00:26:32.260 --> 00:26:33.020
cetera.

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:36.700
And then doing the charcoal
trainings/creative capacity

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:38.140
building as appropriate.

00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:40.390
So I want to talk a little
bit about creative capacity

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:43.840
building, which is a term that
Amy Smith coined for the way

00:26:43.840 --> 00:26:47.950
that she hopes that all
D-Lab groups can work.

00:26:47.950 --> 00:26:52.180
So I want to talk about what she
did at a refugee camp in Uganda

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:54.230
as an example.

00:26:54.230 --> 00:26:58.370
So this is a relief camp
for refugees in Uganda.

00:26:58.370 --> 00:27:02.500
It looks kind of picturesque,
but each one of those huts

00:27:02.500 --> 00:27:04.750
is about 10 or 12
feet in diameter

00:27:04.750 --> 00:27:06.640
and houses 20 people, generally.

00:27:06.640 --> 00:27:09.890
And they've been living
there for 20 to 30 years.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:11.890
So there's plenty of
people who have grown up

00:27:11.890 --> 00:27:14.740
in this refugee camp.

00:27:14.740 --> 00:27:17.110
Their choice is to be there
or to be completely unsafe

00:27:17.110 --> 00:27:19.810
if they're not in the refugee
camp, either getting killed

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:22.247
or getting enlisted
into the army.

00:27:22.247 --> 00:27:23.830
And because they're
in a camp, there's

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:26.340
really no natural resources
or ability to do work.

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:28.510
So there's a real
culture of dependency.

00:27:28.510 --> 00:27:31.540
Things are finally safe enough
that this group of people is

00:27:31.540 --> 00:27:34.300
getting-- and it's
about 100,000 people--

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:37.570
group of people is getting
ready to get repatriated back

00:27:37.570 --> 00:27:40.390
to their original homes.

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:42.920
But people have basically
lived there their whole lives.

00:27:42.920 --> 00:27:45.170
And so there's this culture
of dependency-- how are we

00:27:45.170 --> 00:27:46.570
going to adjust back to home?

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:49.210
They're used to a very
centralized life in camp,

00:27:49.210 --> 00:27:50.290
and adjusting back.

00:27:50.290 --> 00:27:53.415
And especially the burden on the
women going back is very high.

00:27:53.415 --> 00:27:54.790
Because all of a
sudden, they now

00:27:54.790 --> 00:27:57.220
have to be figuring out
how to cook all the food,

00:27:57.220 --> 00:27:59.773
get all the water, get
all the wood, et cetera.

00:27:59.773 --> 00:28:01.690
And there's been very
little capacity building

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:04.850
in this camp up to now.

00:28:04.850 --> 00:28:09.670
So what Amy did when she was
invited there was initially

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:11.260
do a technology
demonstration with

00:28:11.260 --> 00:28:14.322
a classic D-Lab technology,
which is corn shellers.

00:28:14.322 --> 00:28:16.780
And if you're curious about
them, they're on the back shelf

00:28:16.780 --> 00:28:18.363
back there, so you
can check them out.

00:28:18.363 --> 00:28:25.710
But basically,
the corn sheller--

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:28.060
people generally
shell corn by hand.

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:30.215
It's really labor intensive
and relatively painful

00:28:30.215 --> 00:28:32.340
to do because the corn is
dry, and it's really hard

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:33.660
to get off.

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:37.430
And so D-Lab identified this
injection-molded corn sheller,

00:28:37.430 --> 00:28:39.930
something that can work in a
lot of communities really well,

00:28:39.930 --> 00:28:42.550
but it's really hard to
injection mold at the community

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:43.050
level.

00:28:43.050 --> 00:28:45.633
It requires really high pressure
and plastic, neither of which

00:28:45.633 --> 00:28:46.450
are available.

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:49.500
And so D-Lab developed two ways
of making that corn sheller,

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:52.110
either by welding or
by bending sheet metal,

00:28:52.110 --> 00:28:55.380
that are really easy to make
basically anywhere, and allow

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:59.220
people to shell their own corn
very quickly, very affordably.

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:00.640
And people really loved it.

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:03.180
So they're really excited.

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:06.300
And so this is Kofi,
who traveled with Amy,

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:08.322
and another staff member.

00:29:08.322 --> 00:29:10.530
They taught them how to make
their own corn shellers,

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:11.970
and people are really pumped.

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:13.980
And so this is both
just a tech transfer,

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:15.850
like doing a charcoal
demonstration.

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:18.030
I'm teaching you how to
make these corn shellers,

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:19.920
and hopefully, you can start
making them for yourselves

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:20.730
and using them.

00:29:20.730 --> 00:29:21.390
Awesome.

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:24.820
But also to start inspiring
conversation about,

00:29:24.820 --> 00:29:25.770
what else do you need?

00:29:25.770 --> 00:29:30.610
And how could those needs
be addressed together?

00:29:30.610 --> 00:29:32.700
And so one of the projects
that was discussed

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:36.360
was grinding, so
grinding their corn.

00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:38.070
This is a traditional
grinding stone.

00:29:38.070 --> 00:29:40.320
You can imagine how hard
it is to use this stone

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:43.840
to successfully grind dried
corn, how labor-intensive it

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:44.340
is.

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:46.500
And people found it--

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:47.880
or actually, not
corn, excuse me.

00:29:47.880 --> 00:29:49.560
They grind sesame
and groundnuts,

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:50.970
which are peanuts, in it.

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:53.530
And again, it's very
labor intensive.

00:29:53.530 --> 00:29:56.910
So that was the
issue posed, was how

00:29:56.910 --> 00:30:00.550
do we grind our sesame
and peanuts more easily?

00:30:00.550 --> 00:30:03.660
And so first they looked at
what the commercial method

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:06.360
is in that area is a
commercial grinder, which

00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:10.260
requires diesel and is
totally unaffordable

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:11.950
for this community.

00:30:11.950 --> 00:30:14.680
So they said, all right,
that's not going to work.

00:30:14.680 --> 00:30:16.120
So let's design our own.

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:19.080
And so in a day, they
found a meat grinder,

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:23.880
and put together this really
basic, crappy prototype.

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:26.400
And partly it was crappy
because they had a day,

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:29.220
and partly it was
crappy intentionally

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:32.070
because they built this
with community partners.

00:30:32.070 --> 00:30:33.445
Amy and Kofi really
took the lead

00:30:33.445 --> 00:30:35.820
but working very closely with
the local builders, as well

00:30:35.820 --> 00:30:36.790
as the local people.

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:38.520
And so people got
a really good sense

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:41.970
of how one could develop
this project really fast,

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:45.540
without the fancy MIT education
that Amy brings to the table.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:47.040
But just they got
their head around,

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:50.190
oh, this is how I could do
this, and got really excited.

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:52.500
And they also saw how
relatively crappy this was,

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:53.670
and how there need
to be improvements.

00:30:53.670 --> 00:30:55.045
And so they started
saying things

00:30:55.045 --> 00:30:56.940
like, "When I build
mine, I'm going

00:30:56.940 --> 00:31:00.420
to do this," which is a really
good indicator that they're now

00:31:00.420 --> 00:31:01.980
feeling empowered
to build their own,

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:04.313
rather than when you come
back and build me another one,

00:31:04.313 --> 00:31:05.700
it's going to look like this.

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:07.980
And that's the goal,
is when I build mine

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:11.100
rather than when you come
back and build me mine.

00:31:11.100 --> 00:31:17.170
So people got really excited
and just tried it out.

00:31:17.170 --> 00:31:20.650
And it was a great outcome.

00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:24.750
So this experience helped
Amy solidify her thinking

00:31:24.750 --> 00:31:26.640
about creative capacity
building, which

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:28.400
is we're not there
just for tech transfer,

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:30.900
we're not there just to identify
problems and then come back

00:31:30.900 --> 00:31:33.240
to MIT and fix them, but
working with people side

00:31:33.240 --> 00:31:36.270
by side through all areas
of the design process

00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:39.540
to help them develop their
own solutions to problems

00:31:39.540 --> 00:31:41.010
and work together with them.

00:31:41.010 --> 00:31:43.200
And the nature of
a D-Lab trip, going

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:45.720
for a week in Spring
Break, is we can't fully

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:46.740
achieve all of this.

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:49.890
But at least if we have this
in mind, we can achieve as much

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:52.240
of it as possible.

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:54.600
Some of the amazing things
that happened on this trip

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:58.110
is that the translator
they were working with

00:31:58.110 --> 00:32:01.140
told them that they were
the first foreigners

00:32:01.140 --> 00:32:03.690
that this camp had ever
seen who actually did work.

00:32:03.690 --> 00:32:05.790
Because most people
who come to do

00:32:05.790 --> 00:32:07.560
relief work come
with clipboards,

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:10.780
and do a lot of interviews,
and ask a lot of questions.

00:32:10.780 --> 00:32:14.070
This is not to attack
all relief work.

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:16.090
There's a lot of great
work that goes on.

00:32:16.090 --> 00:32:18.360
But there's also a lot of
clipboard question asking.

00:32:18.360 --> 00:32:19.810
And there's a need for that.

00:32:19.810 --> 00:32:22.650
But actually having
people come and do work,

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:26.680
and work side by side,
is pretty valuable.

00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:30.767
So for an example, here's Kofi
chopping wood for making fires,

00:32:30.767 --> 00:32:32.350
because that's what
needed to be done.

00:32:32.350 --> 00:32:33.640
So he helped out doing it.

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:35.182
And that's the kind
of thing that you

00:32:35.182 --> 00:32:40.170
might start to do when you're
in your community next week.

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:43.530
However, you don't see a
picture of Amy chopping wood,

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:46.500
but you do see a picture
of Amy helping sweep up

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:48.180
after the demonstration.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:49.240
And why is that?

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:51.427
Well, it's totally culturally
inappropriate for Amy

00:32:51.427 --> 00:32:53.760
to chop wood, just as it would
be inappropriate for Kofi

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:54.820
to help sweep up.

00:32:54.820 --> 00:32:57.150
And so while it's great to
break down gender barriers,

00:32:57.150 --> 00:32:59.160
at some level, you also
need to be respectful.

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:01.387
And so Amy will
happily chop firewood

00:33:01.387 --> 00:33:03.970
when given the opportunity, but
if it's totally inappropriate,

00:33:03.970 --> 00:33:05.620
she won't, and vice versa.

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:09.450
So it's a balancing act
about helping people,

00:33:09.450 --> 00:33:12.840
but also being respectful
of what's appropriate.

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:15.180
So it can be very
easy, in communities,

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:16.410
to let people just help you.

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:19.135
People will be really kind,
and gracious, and serve you.

00:33:19.135 --> 00:33:21.510
And it's really important to
make sure that you're acting

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:24.060
as a community member
working, rather than someone

00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:26.640
who's a visitor or guest--
even though you are--

00:33:26.640 --> 00:33:30.180
only being helped.

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:32.850
The general rule of thumb
that Amy likes to say

00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:34.000
is, "People are people."

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:36.810
So if you would help someone
out here doing something,

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:39.150
you should do it
there, and vice versa.

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:42.890
If you'd be really
uncomfortable helping x here,

00:33:42.890 --> 00:33:45.180
then you don't need to
force yourself to be

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:47.670
super uncomfortable there.

00:33:47.670 --> 00:33:52.290
Some of the advantages of doing
this sort of participatory work

00:33:52.290 --> 00:33:54.060
are it's efficient.

00:33:54.060 --> 00:33:56.730
And that's a deceptive
thing to say it's efficient,

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:58.510
because it takes
a really long time

00:33:58.510 --> 00:33:59.760
and can be really frustrating.

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:02.430
However, if you're delivering
a product that no one actually

00:34:02.430 --> 00:34:04.380
needs or is excited
about, then there's

00:34:04.380 --> 00:34:06.930
a real loss of efficiency
there by not working with them

00:34:06.930 --> 00:34:08.460
in the first place.

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:10.679
And similarly, it can be
effective to really work

00:34:10.679 --> 00:34:13.380
with people and get something
that's really valuable.

00:34:13.380 --> 00:34:16.260
It can help transition people
to a level of self-reliance

00:34:16.260 --> 00:34:19.590
they didn't have previously
and help your projects

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:21.989
and the work become sustainable.

00:34:21.989 --> 00:34:24.420
Some of the disadvantages
are it's slow.

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:26.469
It takes a long time.

00:34:26.469 --> 00:34:28.980
It requires a lot of human
and material resources,

00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:29.790
potentially.

00:34:29.790 --> 00:34:31.350
There's a lot of
unpredictability,

00:34:31.350 --> 00:34:33.389
as most teams have
already experienced,

00:34:33.389 --> 00:34:36.090
and if you haven't,
you will experience.

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:37.590
And you have to be
able to just roll

00:34:37.590 --> 00:34:39.630
with that unpredictability,
rather than getting

00:34:39.630 --> 00:34:40.810
super frustrated by it.

00:34:40.810 --> 00:34:42.643
I mean, you will get
super frustrated by it,

00:34:42.643 --> 00:34:47.190
but not letting that frustration
be the dominant experience.

00:34:47.190 --> 00:34:50.070
And then finally,
managing expectations,

00:34:50.070 --> 00:34:54.719
both of not getting your
community partners thinking

00:34:54.719 --> 00:34:57.240
that you're going to do
something magical that isn't

00:34:57.240 --> 00:34:59.700
actually within your
power, or vice versa,

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:02.160
you yourself thinking you're
going to be doing something

00:35:02.160 --> 00:35:04.950
amazing that's actually
not reasonable to expect

00:35:04.950 --> 00:35:07.155
in the time and with
the resources you have.

00:35:13.210 --> 00:35:17.730
So the typical design process
is trying to go from a problem

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:18.600
to a solution.

00:35:18.600 --> 00:35:22.350
And you generally go through
generating ideas, evaluating

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:25.960
them, designing in more detail,
actually building, testing,

00:35:25.960 --> 00:35:26.910
and evaluation.

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:29.010
It's not actually this
linear, of course.

00:35:29.010 --> 00:35:30.850
But that's the general idea.

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:38.550
And so traditionally, you
would involve communities just

00:35:38.550 --> 00:35:39.930
at the end.

00:35:39.930 --> 00:35:42.660
And that's obviously not
ideal, for all the reasons

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:43.717
we've talked about.

00:35:43.717 --> 00:35:46.050
And so then hopefully you
involve them in the beginning.

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:48.900
And the idea of
co-creation is that you're

00:35:48.900 --> 00:35:51.080
involving your community
throughout all stages.

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:55.770
And so one thing to talk
about when you are there is,

00:35:55.770 --> 00:35:58.140
I'm not going to be here
for the next few weeks

00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:00.060
while I'm doing all these steps.

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:01.680
How can I stay
connected with you?

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:03.840
What's the best way to
be in contact with you,

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:06.922
so that you can
involve your community

00:36:06.922 --> 00:36:08.130
partners as much as possible.

00:36:10.685 --> 00:36:12.310
Are there any questions
about all that?

00:36:15.610 --> 00:36:16.930
Yeah.

00:36:16.930 --> 00:36:20.380
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] we have
Lindsay to be with the people

00:36:20.380 --> 00:36:21.730
there.

00:36:21.730 --> 00:36:26.140
AMY BANZAERT: So everyone--
so Lindsay is both really

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:28.990
useful as a go-between,
but she also

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:32.770
can't answer all the questions
that you are going to have.

00:36:32.770 --> 00:36:36.760
Because Lindsay is one of the
organizers of Grupo Phoenix.

00:36:36.760 --> 00:36:39.370
And so Lindsay may
be the person you

00:36:39.370 --> 00:36:41.860
need to use to get the
answers from the women

00:36:41.860 --> 00:36:43.720
you're working with,
but you can't just

00:36:43.720 --> 00:36:45.820
rely on Lindsay, reasonably.

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:50.490
But she may be the best
way to get information.

00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:53.420
So speaking of
information, I want

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:55.940
to talk a little bit about
what the barriers are

00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:58.770
to getting good information,
and some of the methods.

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:01.370
So what makes getting
good information hard?

00:37:01.370 --> 00:37:04.590
And what would you do
to get good information?

00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:10.040
And by information, I mean
learning is this solar cooker

00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:12.440
design actually a good idea?

00:37:12.440 --> 00:37:15.330
How often do you cook with
the solar cooker, et cetera,

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:16.680
et cetera.

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:17.493
Yeah.

00:37:17.493 --> 00:37:18.562
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:18.562 --> 00:37:19.770
AMY BANZAERT: Language, yeah.

00:37:27.100 --> 00:37:27.600
What else?

00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:31.950
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:37:31.950 --> 00:37:33.111
AMY BANZAERT: Whichever.

00:37:33.111 --> 00:37:40.560
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
iteration of [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:42.914
to make sure you do
those things [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:46.220 --> 00:37:47.716
that would be [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:52.723 --> 00:37:54.515
AMY BANZAERT: Well, I
think both, actually.

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:06.020
What else?

00:38:06.020 --> 00:38:06.540
Cultural?

00:38:06.540 --> 00:38:07.040
Yeah.

00:38:14.340 --> 00:38:15.440
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:37.865 --> 00:38:39.990
AUDIENCE: I think sometimes
you might run into some

00:38:39.990 --> 00:38:42.130
people say one thing,
some say another.

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:44.192
So have an idea [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:50.150 --> 00:38:52.417
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, absolutely.

00:38:52.417 --> 00:38:54.500
AUDIENCE: I think time
[INAUDIBLE] that we're only

00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:55.380
there for one week.

00:38:55.380 --> 00:38:56.510
And we're all very
ambitious [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:56.510 --> 00:38:57.950
want to get [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:57.950 --> 00:39:01.890
And maybe we'll want to
bypass small, little things,

00:39:01.890 --> 00:39:03.788
and then they make
a big difference--

00:39:03.788 --> 00:39:05.330
like introducing
ourselves and making

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:07.070
that establishment
of personal contact

00:39:07.070 --> 00:39:09.608
that this guy didn't have.

00:39:09.608 --> 00:39:10.400
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:13.160
Yeah, you can get much
better information

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:15.740
from people if you actually
spend the time to do

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:16.520
the introductions.

00:39:16.520 --> 00:39:18.740
And thankfully for
you guys, you're

00:39:18.740 --> 00:39:21.980
not in a community where it's
traditional to spend an hour

00:39:21.980 --> 00:39:24.110
doing the introductions.

00:39:24.110 --> 00:39:25.940
And how is your family,
and this and that.

00:39:25.940 --> 00:39:27.680
In Nicaragua, at
least in these areas,

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:29.930
people are used to doing
things a little more quickly.

00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:33.200
But doing the Steve Ray
approach-- not his approach,

00:39:33.200 --> 00:39:34.352
but what he did--

00:39:34.352 --> 00:39:35.810
barging right in,
and be, like, oh,

00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:38.537
let me see your shoelaces--
is probably not the best.

00:39:38.537 --> 00:39:41.120
Spending the time to introduce
yourself and get to know people

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:42.828
a little bit is
definitely going to help.

00:39:46.930 --> 00:39:47.605
OK, so--

00:39:47.605 --> 00:39:48.970
AUDIENCE: Gender.

00:39:48.970 --> 00:39:50.410
AMY BANZAERT: Gender, yeah.

00:39:54.070 --> 00:39:57.190
AUDIENCE: I wonder how
very much of a man or woman

00:39:57.190 --> 00:39:59.102
kind of thing.

00:39:59.102 --> 00:40:00.810
AMY BANZAERT: It
depends who you talk to,

00:40:00.810 --> 00:40:03.930
but I would say it's not
as extreme as other places.

00:40:03.930 --> 00:40:05.820
AUDIENCE: Is age
going to be a problem?

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:08.028
Are they going to have
trouble respecting people that

00:40:08.028 --> 00:40:09.015
are younger than them?

00:40:09.015 --> 00:40:10.140
AMY BANZAERT: I don't know.

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:10.740
It could be.

00:40:10.740 --> 00:40:14.530
I didn't encounter
it as a big issue,

00:40:14.530 --> 00:40:16.800
but it's always a possibility.

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:26.090
So some of the issues I have
encountered myself are--

00:40:26.090 --> 00:40:28.910
yes, the language, and sometimes
you deal with the translator,

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:30.285
whether it's
someone on your team

00:40:30.285 --> 00:40:34.250
or someone in your community
organization is helping.

00:40:34.250 --> 00:40:35.990
Educational backgrounds
are different.

00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:37.550
Cultural backgrounds
are different.

00:40:37.550 --> 00:40:39.235
People tell you what
you want to hear,

00:40:39.235 --> 00:40:40.610
what they think
you want to hear,

00:40:40.610 --> 00:40:43.070
as opposed to what you
actually want to hear.

00:40:43.070 --> 00:40:45.990
And you may be easily
talking to the wrong person.

00:40:45.990 --> 00:40:48.080
So to get into those
a little more--

00:40:48.080 --> 00:40:51.050
language, obviously,
is challenging.

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:53.240
And when you use a
translator, there's

00:40:53.240 --> 00:40:57.770
often times where you ask
a question that takes you

00:40:57.770 --> 00:40:59.960
two minutes to say,
and the translator

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:02.630
asks a one-second question.

00:41:02.630 --> 00:41:06.050
And then the person responds
by talking for three minutes,

00:41:06.050 --> 00:41:09.560
and the translator says, "Yes."

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:11.540
And that can be
really frustrating.

00:41:11.540 --> 00:41:13.670
And at the same time,
you're completely

00:41:13.670 --> 00:41:15.080
beholden to your translator.

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:17.135
So if you take them
outside and yell at them--

00:41:17.135 --> 00:41:19.010
I mean, I don't think
that's going to happen.

00:41:19.010 --> 00:41:22.700
But if you took a really
pissed-off approach to this,

00:41:22.700 --> 00:41:29.120
the effect would be even worse
response from your translator.

00:41:29.120 --> 00:41:31.160
Educational
backgrounds-- so what

00:41:31.160 --> 00:41:33.140
I mean by that is
that most of us

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:36.110
are pretty used to talking
in Watts, in Joules,

00:41:36.110 --> 00:41:39.990
in inches, whatever, in units,
and just with a math sense.

00:41:39.990 --> 00:41:41.810
And if you haven't
had a math education,

00:41:41.810 --> 00:41:43.820
you may have a really
intuitive understanding

00:41:43.820 --> 00:41:45.890
of all those concepts,
but you don't actually

00:41:45.890 --> 00:41:48.810
have the same language.

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:51.500
So it's not a Spanish
versus English barrier,

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:53.570
it's an educational
language barrier.

00:41:53.570 --> 00:41:57.240
And so it's beholden
on you when you say,

00:41:57.240 --> 00:42:00.230
how long is that, expecting
3 inches, and someone

00:42:00.230 --> 00:42:04.340
answers some other
analogy, figuring out

00:42:04.340 --> 00:42:06.360
how to translate that
rather than saying,

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:07.818
well, is that 6
inches or 8 inches?

00:42:07.818 --> 00:42:10.360
Because that's not going to give
you very useful information.

00:42:10.360 --> 00:42:12.590
AUDIENCE: And they do
centimeters [INAUDIBLE]..

00:42:12.590 --> 00:42:13.860
AMY BANZAERT: I saw both.

00:42:13.860 --> 00:42:14.568
AUDIENCE: Really?

00:42:14.568 --> 00:42:16.970
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:20.510
But a lot of it is without
standard units, too.

00:42:20.510 --> 00:42:23.810
A lot of people just aren't used
to units and units of measure,

00:42:23.810 --> 00:42:26.420
like what you're used to.

00:42:26.420 --> 00:42:29.293
Cultural background,
of course, and then

00:42:29.293 --> 00:42:30.710
telling you what
you want to hear.

00:42:30.710 --> 00:42:32.120
It's really common
if you say, so

00:42:32.120 --> 00:42:34.125
what do you think of
my design, they'll be,

00:42:34.125 --> 00:42:35.000
like, oh, it's great.

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:36.260
Yeah, we'll totally use that.

00:42:36.260 --> 00:42:36.770
Awesome.

00:42:36.770 --> 00:42:37.610
Love it.

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:39.140
Yeah, it's good.

00:42:39.140 --> 00:42:43.280
And unless you both ask
your question more carefully

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:45.630
and keep probing, you'll
never hear beyond that.

00:42:45.630 --> 00:42:48.200
And it's really easy when
you're proud of something to be,

00:42:48.200 --> 00:42:50.270
like, well they
said they love it.

00:42:50.270 --> 00:42:51.140
Awesome.

00:42:51.140 --> 00:42:53.940
And so working through
that is important.

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:55.830
And then also talking
to the wrong person--

00:42:55.830 --> 00:42:58.640
so it can be really easy to ask
all your questions of Lindsay,

00:42:58.640 --> 00:43:00.800
for example, as the
community organizer.

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:02.813
And she speaks
English, et cetera.

00:43:02.813 --> 00:43:04.730
And she'll give you some
really good insights,

00:43:04.730 --> 00:43:06.230
but if you just talk
to her, you won't

00:43:06.230 --> 00:43:07.370
get the right information.

00:43:07.370 --> 00:43:09.650
Or you can interview someone
about cooking habits,

00:43:09.650 --> 00:43:12.350
and then if you forget to ask,
how often do you cook, and find

00:43:12.350 --> 00:43:13.820
out actually they
aren't the cook,

00:43:13.820 --> 00:43:15.890
but they'll answer it because
you're asking these questions--

00:43:15.890 --> 00:43:17.432
if you don't ask
the right questions,

00:43:17.432 --> 00:43:19.650
you're just never going to
get the right information.

00:43:19.650 --> 00:43:21.080
And there's classic
examples of that

00:43:21.080 --> 00:43:23.210
in terms of talking to the
male leaders in the community

00:43:23.210 --> 00:43:25.250
and never talking to the
women in the community.

00:43:25.250 --> 00:43:27.440
I think that'll be less of an
issue for most of you guys,

00:43:27.440 --> 00:43:28.730
given who you're working with.

00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:31.410
But it's something
to keep in mind.

00:43:31.410 --> 00:43:33.900
So I got to this, and
you got to it, too--

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:38.780
so some of the methods to manage
this are asking the same person

00:43:38.780 --> 00:43:42.230
the same question in many
different ways, multiple times,

00:43:42.230 --> 00:43:44.090
as well as asking
different people the same

00:43:44.090 --> 00:43:46.580
question multiple times,
anticipating that you're

00:43:46.580 --> 00:43:48.870
going to get some variation.

00:43:48.870 --> 00:43:51.680
Another thing you can
do is ask some questions

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:55.970
that you kind of already know
the answer to, if you do,

00:43:55.970 --> 00:44:00.200
so that you can get an indicator
of how reliable this person is

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:02.240
in terms of giving you
useful information.

00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:04.430
Because some people
are much more on the

00:44:04.430 --> 00:44:07.310
telling you what they think you
want to hear side than others.

00:44:07.310 --> 00:44:08.990
And there's some
probing questions

00:44:08.990 --> 00:44:10.910
you might be able
to develop that

00:44:10.910 --> 00:44:13.190
can help you get around that.

00:44:13.190 --> 00:44:15.320
Demonstrating an
openness to criticism--

00:44:15.320 --> 00:44:17.757
and that generally does
not mean saying, oh, you

00:44:17.757 --> 00:44:20.090
won't offend me, tell me
whatever you want, but can mean

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:23.420
something like, I've talked to
some people who've criticized

00:44:23.420 --> 00:44:27.470
or have said they don't like
this idea because of this.

00:44:27.470 --> 00:44:30.710
Is that something that you
think might be a problem, too?

00:44:30.710 --> 00:44:32.250
Why or why not?

00:44:32.250 --> 00:44:36.350
And just repeatedly attacking
yourself so that you actually

00:44:36.350 --> 00:44:37.940
get some criticisms from them.

00:44:37.940 --> 00:44:40.070
Because as great
as you all are, I'm

00:44:40.070 --> 00:44:43.380
sure there's going to be
some flaws in every approach.

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:45.230
And so getting to
those flaws helps

00:44:45.230 --> 00:44:48.770
you get to a really
great product faster.

00:44:48.770 --> 00:44:51.290
Repeating answers back
is a classic method

00:44:51.290 --> 00:44:54.830
for making sure that there's
understanding on both sides.

00:44:54.830 --> 00:44:57.590
Encouraging questions,
very explicitly saying

00:44:57.590 --> 00:45:00.260
what questions do you have
for me, what can I tell you,

00:45:00.260 --> 00:45:02.300
is pretty important.

00:45:02.300 --> 00:45:04.540
Verifying expertise--
so as I mentioned

00:45:04.540 --> 00:45:07.040
with the wrong person, if you
talk to someone about cooking,

00:45:07.040 --> 00:45:08.990
actually making
sure that they cook

00:45:08.990 --> 00:45:11.520
would be a good thing to do.

00:45:11.520 --> 00:45:13.670
And then finally, dealing
with the translator,

00:45:13.670 --> 00:45:16.040
spending some time
beforehand talking

00:45:16.040 --> 00:45:19.040
about what your goals are,
what your preferences are.

00:45:19.040 --> 00:45:21.080
Because translators
have choices in terms

00:45:21.080 --> 00:45:23.690
of do they give you the
detailed response or do

00:45:23.690 --> 00:45:24.920
they just give you the yes?

00:45:24.920 --> 00:45:27.337
And if you're on a time crunch,
you may just want the yes,

00:45:27.337 --> 00:45:28.940
but if you have the
time, you probably

00:45:28.940 --> 00:45:32.450
want the detailed answer
with the subtleties,

00:45:32.450 --> 00:45:34.640
even if it's more
painful to translate.

00:45:38.410 --> 00:45:42.100
So one of the
challenges when you're

00:45:42.100 --> 00:45:46.000
working on engineering projects
is getting to the core problem.

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:49.540
And that ties in to the
project specifications.

00:45:49.540 --> 00:45:52.630
So someone will say,
I need a new stove,

00:45:52.630 --> 00:45:56.380
instead of I need to not
have such a smoky environment

00:45:56.380 --> 00:45:57.185
in my kitchen.

00:45:57.185 --> 00:45:58.810
And you need to make
sure you're asking

00:45:58.810 --> 00:46:02.860
the questions to get to "smoky
environment," not "new stove."

00:46:02.860 --> 00:46:05.440
It would be really easy to
specify that brand-new stove

00:46:05.440 --> 00:46:09.920
without getting the
right question answered,

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:11.440
which is the smoky environment.

00:46:11.440 --> 00:46:15.790
So partly, it's making sure that
you're not letting them design

00:46:15.790 --> 00:46:17.080
for you, which everyone does.

00:46:17.080 --> 00:46:18.455
It's really hard
to describe what

00:46:18.455 --> 00:46:20.080
you need in terms
of specifications,

00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:23.350
as you probably just found
out, or saw in that 2-level 9

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:25.240
lecture I linked to
when trying to describe

00:46:25.240 --> 00:46:29.230
a lemon or a piece of fruit
as a product specification,

00:46:29.230 --> 00:46:31.540
rather than, "I need a lemon."

00:46:31.540 --> 00:46:32.800
That's much harder.

00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:34.890
But this is actually
the information

00:46:34.890 --> 00:46:37.390
you need to make a good design
so that you're not going down

00:46:37.390 --> 00:46:40.240
the path of a design
that may not actually

00:46:40.240 --> 00:46:43.000
match the real needs,
because it's easier

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:45.670
to think in products
than in specifications.

00:46:48.480 --> 00:46:50.490
So getting to the core problem--

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:53.700
some ways you can do that, in
addition to just being patient

00:46:53.700 --> 00:46:56.670
when people talk about what
they want in terms of a product

00:46:56.670 --> 00:46:59.040
and then you translating
back into the specifications,

00:46:59.040 --> 00:47:03.002
is, "Tell me what would happen
when you use this new thing.

00:47:03.002 --> 00:47:04.960
Tell me a story about
what that would be like."

00:47:04.960 --> 00:47:06.840
And have them walk
you through what life

00:47:06.840 --> 00:47:09.762
is like with this new option.

00:47:09.762 --> 00:47:11.220
When you're trying
to find out more

00:47:11.220 --> 00:47:14.410
about what the specifications
are, rather than saying,

00:47:14.410 --> 00:47:17.460
how big should it be, saying,
well, how big is too big?

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:19.860
Is 10 feet-- is
this big too big?

00:47:19.860 --> 00:47:20.700
Is this big too big?

00:47:20.700 --> 00:47:22.290
And again, I just
said, "10 feet,"

00:47:22.290 --> 00:47:23.610
and that's probably not
what you want to say.

00:47:23.610 --> 00:47:25.290
You want to use your
hands to gesture

00:47:25.290 --> 00:47:27.060
or use some other
things to compare.

00:47:27.060 --> 00:47:29.340
Because 10 feet, even
for us, is a little hard

00:47:29.340 --> 00:47:31.290
to imagine exactly
how big 10 feet is.

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:33.000
And for someone
who hasn't grown up

00:47:33.000 --> 00:47:37.690
using rulers and tape measures,
et cetera, it's harder.

00:47:37.690 --> 00:47:39.900
And so I put "big" and
"small" in parentheses

00:47:39.900 --> 00:47:42.420
because it could be
like how hot, how cold,

00:47:42.420 --> 00:47:45.420
how whatever-- all sorts
of different things.

00:47:45.420 --> 00:47:47.430
Challenge their requests.

00:47:47.430 --> 00:47:53.620
So if they say, oh, no, it can't
be bigger than this, say, well,

00:47:53.620 --> 00:47:55.200
what would happen
if it was this big?

00:47:55.200 --> 00:47:56.550
Why would that be a bad thing?

00:47:56.550 --> 00:47:59.220
So that you really understand
what they're getting at.

00:47:59.220 --> 00:48:02.400
And then getting feedback
on your own ideas,

00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:04.720
including ones that
are problematic,

00:48:04.720 --> 00:48:07.560
so showing them a design
that you know some issues

00:48:07.560 --> 00:48:09.270
and getting feedback
on it, trying

00:48:09.270 --> 00:48:12.858
to get them comfortable with
criticizing so that you can--

00:48:12.858 --> 00:48:15.150
and then also listening to
the way they're criticizing.

00:48:15.150 --> 00:48:17.940
What they're catching
maybe opens up

00:48:17.940 --> 00:48:20.470
some information for you.

00:48:20.470 --> 00:48:23.100
And then finally, as you're
engaged in these questions

00:48:23.100 --> 00:48:27.525
and conversations, taking time
mentally to review what you

00:48:27.525 --> 00:48:29.828
know and do a mental design
process of writing down

00:48:29.828 --> 00:48:32.370
the specifications, and being,
like, OK, I know these things.

00:48:32.370 --> 00:48:33.388
What else don't I know?

00:48:33.388 --> 00:48:35.430
So that you make sure to
get to all the questions

00:48:35.430 --> 00:48:36.450
you need to ask.

00:48:36.450 --> 00:48:38.760
And it's kind of hard
and takes practice,

00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:42.990
but you guys will do great.

00:48:42.990 --> 00:48:49.440
So now, I'm going to save
that till Friday because we're

00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:50.430
running out of time.

00:48:53.380 --> 00:48:55.065
So I just wanted to go over--

00:48:55.065 --> 00:48:56.940
or maybe I'll get back
to it if I have time--

00:48:56.940 --> 00:49:02.130
the packing list and
trip advice, which is all

00:49:02.130 --> 00:49:02.960
posted on Stellar.

00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:04.960
But in case you haven't
had a chance to read it,

00:49:04.960 --> 00:49:06.720
I know they're kind of long.

00:49:06.720 --> 00:49:08.700
So some people were
earlier asking me,

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:10.920
do I need to buy a new x?

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:12.420
Probably not.

00:49:12.420 --> 00:49:14.520
There might be one or two
things you want to buy,

00:49:14.520 --> 00:49:16.830
but generally, the
goal of D-Lab is not

00:49:16.830 --> 00:49:20.830
to make yourself broke going to
travel to a developing country.

00:49:20.830 --> 00:49:22.590
So very little needs
to be bought new.

00:49:22.590 --> 00:49:25.080
And pack lightly, which
is in direct contradiction

00:49:25.080 --> 00:49:27.540
to the two-page packing
list that I provided you.

00:49:27.540 --> 00:49:29.310
But basically, that
two-page packing list

00:49:29.310 --> 00:49:31.890
is the list of all things
that someone at D-Lab

00:49:31.890 --> 00:49:32.880
really likes bringing.

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:35.430
And so some people really
value certain things,

00:49:35.430 --> 00:49:37.530
other people value other things.

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:40.500
You have to know yourself
what are the objects that

00:49:40.500 --> 00:49:42.360
are going to be
important for you to have

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:43.800
and what are the ones
that you can skip.

00:49:43.800 --> 00:49:45.967
So there's just a couple
that I have on that packing

00:49:45.967 --> 00:49:46.890
list that are in blue.

00:49:46.890 --> 00:49:49.172
I didn't even put underwear
in blue, just the ones

00:49:49.172 --> 00:49:50.880
like a flashlight that
if you don't have,

00:49:50.880 --> 00:49:52.720
it's going to be really
hard for you to function.

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:54.030
So things like that
you have to have.

00:49:54.030 --> 00:49:55.380
Everything else,
that's your call.

00:49:55.380 --> 00:49:57.755
So if you don't have Teva's,
you don't have hiking boots,

00:49:57.755 --> 00:49:59.970
you don't have whatever,
don't worry about it.

00:49:59.970 --> 00:50:02.440
Don't go out and buy yourself
a $200 pair of hiking boots

00:50:02.440 --> 00:50:04.440
that you'll never wear
again just for this trip.

00:50:04.440 --> 00:50:07.450
That's not necessary.

00:50:07.450 --> 00:50:09.030
So pack lightly.

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:12.060
With that being said, it can be
as cold as 50 degrees at night,

00:50:12.060 --> 00:50:14.590
so you might want a light
fleece or something like that.

00:50:14.590 --> 00:50:18.840
And then in the middle of the
day, it's wicked hot and humid.

00:50:18.840 --> 00:50:22.350
Relatively conservative
dress-- again,

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:25.860
for a lot of these things
I'm asking you to be more

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:28.080
conservative than you may
see most people being--

00:50:28.080 --> 00:50:30.930
not most, but many people
in Nicaragua being--

00:50:30.930 --> 00:50:32.520
because again,
we're representing

00:50:32.520 --> 00:50:34.390
MIT, and D-Lab, and yada, yada.

00:50:34.390 --> 00:50:36.450
And so we want to be
respectful to the people who

00:50:36.450 --> 00:50:40.590
are on the conservative side
in terms of dress, et cetera.

00:50:40.590 --> 00:50:43.110
Toiletries-- so if you
have any medications

00:50:43.110 --> 00:50:46.110
that you need to take, you
should take twice as many

00:50:46.110 --> 00:50:49.210
as you need and keep them
in two separate places

00:50:49.210 --> 00:50:53.430
so that if a bag gets stolen
or lost, you have your meds.

00:50:53.430 --> 00:50:55.740
You definitely want to bring
toilet paper, or tissues,

00:50:55.740 --> 00:50:57.907
or something like that you
can use for toilet paper,

00:50:57.907 --> 00:51:00.870
because not all
places will have it.

00:51:00.870 --> 00:51:03.420
You won't be offered
a towel, so if you

00:51:03.420 --> 00:51:05.340
want to have a
towel for showering,

00:51:05.340 --> 00:51:07.390
which will be bucket showers--

00:51:07.390 --> 00:51:09.330
so taking a scoop of
water from a bucket

00:51:09.330 --> 00:51:10.915
and pouring it on your head--

00:51:10.915 --> 00:51:11.790
you should bring one.

00:51:11.790 --> 00:51:14.850
But I would bring a small one
because they're pretty bulky.

00:51:14.850 --> 00:51:16.890
You definitely want bug
repellent and sunscreen.

00:51:16.890 --> 00:51:19.500
The sun is much
stronger, and you

00:51:19.500 --> 00:51:21.570
will sunburn if you
don't wear sunscreen,

00:51:21.570 --> 00:51:23.160
if you're fair skinned.

00:51:23.160 --> 00:51:24.510
Flashlight, again, is key.

00:51:24.510 --> 00:51:28.290
Having a photocopy of your
passport, bringing a notebook,

00:51:28.290 --> 00:51:30.040
like I mentioned.

00:51:30.040 --> 00:51:31.780
And then nothing of value--

00:51:31.780 --> 00:51:34.440
so you shouldn't be
wearing your Brass Rats.

00:51:34.440 --> 00:51:39.300
You could skip earrings or
wear really small, subtle ones.

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:41.700
Cameras, like a small, junky
point-and-shoot is fine,

00:51:41.700 --> 00:51:44.490
but a DSLR is not ideal.

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:47.510
Again, you'll see some people
in Nicaragua with DSLRs,

00:51:47.510 --> 00:51:50.750
but they're making the
choice for themselves

00:51:50.750 --> 00:51:53.040
about having that,
and therefore,

00:51:53.040 --> 00:51:55.220
bringing the potential
crime associated with having

00:51:55.220 --> 00:51:56.540
a fancy device there.

00:51:56.540 --> 00:51:57.920
And it's not fair
for you to make

00:51:57.920 --> 00:52:02.060
that call for your entire
team on that camera.

00:52:02.060 --> 00:52:02.738
Yeah.

00:52:02.738 --> 00:52:04.780
AUDIENCE: What kind of
risk are we talking about?

00:52:04.780 --> 00:52:07.910
[INAUDIBLE] waterproof and
shockproof, or is it theft?

00:52:07.910 --> 00:52:10.533
AMY BANZAERT: Theft.

00:52:10.533 --> 00:52:11.575
AUDIENCE: So even if we--

00:52:11.575 --> 00:52:12.408
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:12.408 --> 00:52:15.237
AMY BANZAERT: So do you have
a waterproof, shockproof DSLR?

00:52:15.237 --> 00:52:16.223
AUDIENCE: What's DSLR?

00:52:16.223 --> 00:52:17.390
AMY BANZAERT: Like the big--

00:52:17.390 --> 00:52:18.798
AUDIENCE: No, not the big one.

00:52:18.798 --> 00:52:20.840
AMY BANZAERT: So a point
and shoot, totally fine.

00:52:20.840 --> 00:52:22.115
Yeah, yeah, so that's
what I'm saying.

00:52:22.115 --> 00:52:22.400
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:22.400 --> 00:52:23.233
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:23.233 --> 00:52:26.630
AMY BANZAERT: Like a camera
that's this size is fine.

00:52:26.630 --> 00:52:32.090
A camera that's that size,
not fine, is what I'm saying.

00:52:32.090 --> 00:52:34.340
Ones that have the
switch-outable lenses

00:52:34.340 --> 00:52:37.100
is a DSLR, fancy, nice camera.

00:52:37.100 --> 00:52:38.870
That's what I'm asking
you not to take.

00:52:38.870 --> 00:52:42.470
Point and shoot you
should totally take.

00:52:42.470 --> 00:52:46.010
And then laptops,
your team probably

00:52:46.010 --> 00:52:47.300
shouldn't bring any laptops.

00:52:47.300 --> 00:52:50.540
If you need to, you should bring
one, but definitely not more

00:52:50.540 --> 00:52:51.600
than that.

00:52:51.600 --> 00:52:52.100
Yeah.

00:52:52.100 --> 00:52:53.510
AUDIENCE: Do you recommend
bringing more than one memory

00:52:53.510 --> 00:52:54.650
card for a camera?

00:52:54.650 --> 00:52:55.400
AMY BANZAERT: Yes.

00:52:55.400 --> 00:52:58.820
So I recommend bringing as much
memory and battery charging

00:52:58.820 --> 00:53:01.970
capability as you can,
both because you'll

00:53:01.970 --> 00:53:03.560
have limited access
to electricity

00:53:03.560 --> 00:53:05.180
for battery charging,
and then you may

00:53:05.180 --> 00:53:06.470
be taking a lot of pictures.

00:53:06.470 --> 00:53:11.090
And you may not have a laptop
and be able to download them

00:53:11.090 --> 00:53:12.020
until you get home.

00:53:15.890 --> 00:53:17.416
I'm not sure why that's--

00:53:17.416 --> 00:53:20.506
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:53:20.506 --> 00:53:21.298
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah?

00:53:21.298 --> 00:53:23.010
AUDIENCE: Cellphones.

00:53:23.010 --> 00:53:25.520
AMY BANZAERT: So
the thing that's

00:53:25.520 --> 00:53:28.130
complicated-- so
every trip leader will

00:53:28.130 --> 00:53:29.670
have a cellphone with them.

00:53:29.670 --> 00:53:32.600
So for emergencies, there's a
way to call the States or a way

00:53:32.600 --> 00:53:36.290
to call within your country,
or whatever you need.

00:53:36.290 --> 00:53:40.700
I can't see why you'd
need a cellphone.

00:53:40.700 --> 00:53:43.953
It might be nice to have during
your layovers or whatever,

00:53:43.953 --> 00:53:45.870
so you might want to
bring it for that reason.

00:53:45.870 --> 00:53:48.080
But if it's anything
at all nice--

00:53:48.080 --> 00:53:53.240
and I'm concluding my
junky phone as nice--

00:53:53.240 --> 00:53:55.700
but anything that looks
fancier than like flip phones

00:53:55.700 --> 00:53:58.850
that people had 10
years ago should never

00:53:58.850 --> 00:54:01.365
be visible in Nicaragua.

00:54:01.365 --> 00:54:02.990
So if you're going
to bring it, pack it

00:54:02.990 --> 00:54:07.100
in the bottom of your bag
as soon as you get to Miami,

00:54:07.100 --> 00:54:09.800
and leave it there rather
than taking it out.

00:54:09.800 --> 00:54:12.020
So again, you'll
see a few people

00:54:12.020 --> 00:54:15.080
with iPhones in Nicaragua,
but they're taking a risk.

00:54:15.080 --> 00:54:17.978
And you shouldn't have yours out
and about while you're there.

00:54:17.978 --> 00:54:19.520
But the one thing
is that most of you

00:54:19.520 --> 00:54:22.070
are leaving super,
super early on Sunday.

00:54:22.070 --> 00:54:25.430
So you do want to have your
cell phone out that morning,

00:54:25.430 --> 00:54:28.268
from that middle of the night,
just to make sure you're awake.

00:54:28.268 --> 00:54:30.560
I don't know, at 3:00 AM, I
feel like most you probably

00:54:30.560 --> 00:54:32.180
won't even sleep right.

00:54:32.180 --> 00:54:35.600
I don't go to sleep if
I have to leave at 3:00.

00:54:35.600 --> 00:54:38.310
But yeah, we want to
make sure you get there.

00:54:38.310 --> 00:54:39.860
So one other thing
you want to bring

00:54:39.860 --> 00:54:42.990
is gifts for your
community partners.

00:54:42.990 --> 00:54:46.970
And some criteria for what
makes a good gift is one,

00:54:46.970 --> 00:54:50.060
that it's not extravagantly
over-the-top expensive

00:54:50.060 --> 00:54:53.895
because it can set up a--

00:54:53.895 --> 00:54:57.350
the disparity between wealth
can be more significant

00:54:57.350 --> 00:54:59.660
and make people
feel uncomfortable.

00:54:59.660 --> 00:55:01.220
Also, you may well
be on a budget,

00:55:01.220 --> 00:55:05.940
and I don't want you to go broke
buying gifts for your family.

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:08.030
Other things are, will
they actually use it?

00:55:08.030 --> 00:55:10.010
Will this just become
some piece of trash

00:55:10.010 --> 00:55:13.790
that adds trash to the
community in places

00:55:13.790 --> 00:55:16.820
where they're relatively
pristine or not?

00:55:16.820 --> 00:55:19.830
But we don't want to be adding
to the trash load, et cetera.

00:55:19.830 --> 00:55:24.290
So D-Lab has a
few things that we

00:55:24.290 --> 00:55:26.540
buy that are branded
as D-Lab objects,

00:55:26.540 --> 00:55:29.022
that you can buy from D-Lab.

00:55:29.022 --> 00:55:30.980
So gifts are not something
that D-Lab pays for.

00:55:30.980 --> 00:55:34.700
It's something that you pay
for as a team or an individual

00:55:34.700 --> 00:55:36.500
because they're from you.

00:55:36.500 --> 00:55:39.030
And so it's up to you
to decide how much,

00:55:39.030 --> 00:55:41.460
but I think something
is appropriate.

00:55:41.460 --> 00:55:45.180
So sports bottles are $5.

00:55:45.180 --> 00:55:48.818
These hats, maybe $6 or $8.

00:55:48.818 --> 00:55:50.360
Hats, I think, are
a pretty good gift

00:55:50.360 --> 00:55:51.568
because people can wear them.

00:55:51.568 --> 00:55:54.530
And they like them, and a lot
of people wear baseball caps.

00:55:54.530 --> 00:55:56.030
But don't bring
them Red Sox caps

00:55:56.030 --> 00:55:58.375
because most of
them like Yankees.

00:55:58.375 --> 00:56:00.500
And those are the only two
teams that are basically

00:56:00.500 --> 00:56:02.120
known, from what I saw.

00:56:02.120 --> 00:56:03.920
We also have these
little flashlights.

00:56:06.930 --> 00:56:09.820
Oh, I think the battery is
blocked so I can't turn it on.

00:56:09.820 --> 00:56:12.420
But yeah, the challenge
with these flashlights

00:56:12.420 --> 00:56:14.170
is it means the battery
has to be replaced

00:56:14.170 --> 00:56:15.045
and things like that.

00:56:15.045 --> 00:56:18.000
So it's a mixed bag,
how valuable it is.

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:20.070
But they're fun.

00:56:20.070 --> 00:56:24.330
There's little drawstring
bags, which people like.

00:56:24.330 --> 00:56:26.730
I often bring chocolate to
share it with my family.

00:56:26.730 --> 00:56:29.852
I'll bring a couple of hats to
bring for someone like Lindsay

00:56:29.852 --> 00:56:32.310
or someone who's helping you
a lot, and then for the family

00:56:32.310 --> 00:56:34.650
just bring chocolate that
you can share with them.

00:56:34.650 --> 00:56:36.900
Trader Joe's sells pretty
good, pretty cheap chocolate

00:56:36.900 --> 00:56:38.490
that travels pretty well.

00:56:38.490 --> 00:56:41.220
Some chocolate melts really
easily and that's not ideal for

00:56:41.220 --> 00:56:43.410
given how hot it is.

00:56:43.410 --> 00:56:46.693
But some of the nicer chocolate
comes in three packs for $2.

00:56:46.693 --> 00:56:49.110
And just sharing that with
your family, it means something

00:56:49.110 --> 00:56:50.812
you can share with
all the members.

00:56:50.812 --> 00:56:52.770
And it's nice, and it's
from your home country.

00:56:52.770 --> 00:56:54.420
Chocolate and Nicaragua,
in my opinion,

00:56:54.420 --> 00:57:00.540
is far inferior to chocolate in
the States and a lot of people

00:57:00.540 --> 00:57:01.980
I know agree.

00:57:01.980 --> 00:57:04.990
And so something like
that is also appropriate.

00:57:04.990 --> 00:57:06.974
So are there any
questions on gifts?

00:57:06.974 --> 00:57:09.317
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] any
of these [INAUDIBLE] gifts?

00:57:09.317 --> 00:57:11.400
AMY BANZAERT: So I'm trying
to leave a little time

00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:13.530
at the end of this class
so that your team can

00:57:13.530 --> 00:57:15.613
make a list of all the
things you need from D-Lab.

00:57:15.613 --> 00:57:17.460
And these gift things
are among them.

00:57:20.010 --> 00:57:22.722
In terms of money
that you might need,

00:57:22.722 --> 00:57:24.180
you should bring
your Charlie card,

00:57:24.180 --> 00:57:26.138
because you'll probably
be coming back on the T

00:57:26.138 --> 00:57:28.200
if not going there.

00:57:28.200 --> 00:57:31.620
There's an entry visa cost
to get into Nicaragua of $10,

00:57:31.620 --> 00:57:33.630
and if you just have
a $10 bill on you,

00:57:33.630 --> 00:57:36.120
it's nice to not have to
deal with the customs agent

00:57:36.120 --> 00:57:37.830
making change.

00:57:37.830 --> 00:57:39.330
If you're bringing cash--

00:57:39.330 --> 00:57:44.123
you may want some cash for food
that's not already prepaid,

00:57:44.123 --> 00:57:46.290
which you guys will reimburse
us for after the trip,

00:57:46.290 --> 00:57:50.190
or for gifts for yourself
to bring home, for yourself

00:57:50.190 --> 00:57:52.440
or your family members
at the airport,

00:57:52.440 --> 00:57:54.430
if not while you're traveling.

00:57:54.430 --> 00:57:57.480
There'll be opportunities
to do a little shopping.

00:57:57.480 --> 00:58:01.950
So you shouldn't bring anything
bigger than a $20 bill.

00:58:01.950 --> 00:58:04.350
And smaller bills
are nicer generally,

00:58:04.350 --> 00:58:06.308
because it's hard for
people to make change.

00:58:06.308 --> 00:58:08.100
But the bill should be
in decent condition.

00:58:08.100 --> 00:58:11.400
If they're really ratty,
people won't take them.

00:58:11.400 --> 00:58:13.890
And then if you
expect to be needing

00:58:13.890 --> 00:58:15.480
to use your bank,
or credit card,

00:58:15.480 --> 00:58:18.720
or debit card either for
general just getting money out

00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:20.450
of the ATM or for
an emergency, you

00:58:20.450 --> 00:58:22.617
should call them to warn
them that you're traveling,

00:58:22.617 --> 00:58:24.330
so that if you do
need it, you can.

00:58:24.330 --> 00:58:26.130
Because usually, they'll
block any charges

00:58:26.130 --> 00:58:27.588
unless you told
them ahead of time.

00:58:31.380 --> 00:58:36.270
Some tips-- so you should assume
that drinking water is unsafe,

00:58:36.270 --> 00:58:39.420
which means that any vegetables
or fruits that aren't cooked

00:58:39.420 --> 00:58:40.200
are also unsafe.

00:58:40.200 --> 00:58:42.575
Because they were probably
washed in that unsafe drinking

00:58:42.575 --> 00:58:43.720
water.

00:58:43.720 --> 00:58:47.952
So each trip leader
will have a water filter

00:58:47.952 --> 00:58:50.160
that you guys can use to
filter your own water, which

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:52.380
means you really do want
to bring a water bottle so

00:58:52.380 --> 00:58:56.040
that you can have clean
water in that water bottle.

00:58:56.040 --> 00:58:59.610
But this is definitely something
to be cautious on so you

00:58:59.610 --> 00:59:00.830
stay healthy for the trip.

00:59:00.830 --> 00:59:02.955
Because it's such a short
trip that if you get sick

00:59:02.955 --> 00:59:05.560
for a few days,
that's half your trip.

00:59:05.560 --> 00:59:09.060
Nothing expensive, at least
in sight or brought, so

00:59:09.060 --> 00:59:12.480
like a Brass Rat, or nice
jewelry, or watch, or whatever,

00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:13.007
leave here.

00:59:13.007 --> 00:59:15.090
If you need to bring that
cellphone so you have it

00:59:15.090 --> 00:59:17.460
while you're in the States
while you're traveling,

00:59:17.460 --> 00:59:22.140
just keep it hidden in your
bag so that it's not being

00:59:22.140 --> 00:59:25.230
brought attention to at all.

00:59:25.230 --> 00:59:28.050
You should always be
traveling in groups, ideally,

00:59:28.050 --> 00:59:29.988
but especially at night.

00:59:29.988 --> 00:59:32.280
There's going to be some
cases where that'll be tricky.

00:59:32.280 --> 00:59:37.420
But do your best to be
as safe as possible.

00:59:37.420 --> 00:59:40.050
As I mentioned,
flexibility and patience

00:59:40.050 --> 00:59:42.600
are really great assets to have.

00:59:42.600 --> 00:59:44.460
So just go in not
expecting to be

00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:47.370
as efficient or effective
as you necessarily hoped.

00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:50.170
And expect that things are going
to change at the last minute.

00:59:50.170 --> 00:59:52.320
And that'll help
make the trip more

00:59:52.320 --> 00:59:54.400
fun rather than frustrating.

00:59:54.400 --> 00:59:57.415
And then managing expectations
with the community partners--

00:59:57.415 --> 00:59:59.040
it's really easy for
community partners

00:59:59.040 --> 01:00:00.640
to be, like, when
are you coming back.

01:00:00.640 --> 01:00:02.100
And you're so pumped, you're
having such a great time,

01:00:02.100 --> 01:00:04.170
and you're, like, I'll
be back this summer,

01:00:04.170 --> 01:00:06.932
not thinking about actually, I
have an internship at whoever,

01:00:06.932 --> 01:00:08.640
and I'm not going to
be back this summer.

01:00:08.640 --> 01:00:09.660
I may never be back.

01:00:09.660 --> 01:00:11.490
And so you don't want
to make any promises

01:00:11.490 --> 01:00:12.780
that you can't keep.

01:00:12.780 --> 01:00:15.540
That's really important because
that can ruin our relationship

01:00:15.540 --> 01:00:16.707
with our community partners.

01:00:19.080 --> 01:00:21.840
So just a reminder, a few of
you still owe me at least some

01:00:21.840 --> 01:00:22.690
of these things.

01:00:22.690 --> 01:00:25.050
And so please get them
to me today or tell me

01:00:25.050 --> 01:00:27.240
why you can't, and what
you're going to do,

01:00:27.240 --> 01:00:29.320
if you haven't already.

01:00:29.320 --> 01:00:34.890
And so for the
remainder of the class,

01:00:34.890 --> 01:00:39.120
I need to do micro interviews
with everyone who's traveling.

01:00:39.120 --> 01:00:40.810
It's just a D-Lab
thing that we do.

01:00:40.810 --> 01:00:42.435
We just talk one on
one really briefly.

01:00:45.180 --> 01:00:47.640
You may want to revise
your plan a little bit

01:00:47.640 --> 01:00:49.615
based on feedback from
the presentations.

01:00:49.615 --> 01:00:51.240
You probably want to
talk in your group

01:00:51.240 --> 01:00:53.910
about how much checked luggage
you're having versus carry on,

01:00:53.910 --> 01:00:56.790
who's carrying what for
any bulky items you have.

01:00:56.790 --> 01:00:58.890
Things that you might
need from D-Lab,

01:00:58.890 --> 01:01:01.390
I need you to tell me by
the end of class today.

01:01:01.390 --> 01:01:03.390
So you might need tools.

01:01:03.390 --> 01:01:04.470
You might need cameras.

01:01:04.470 --> 01:01:08.640
We have some that we can lend,
mosquito nets, hammocks, other.

01:01:08.640 --> 01:01:11.130
It'd be nice if at least
a few people have cameras

01:01:11.130 --> 01:01:14.280
so that you can get
different angles of shots

01:01:14.280 --> 01:01:16.000
when you're all taking
pictures together.

01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:20.610
And also, if one dies, you're
not unable to take pictures.

01:01:20.610 --> 01:01:23.520
Because pictures are really
important for the projects.

01:01:23.520 --> 01:01:26.620
And then anything else
that you might need.

01:01:26.620 --> 01:01:30.690
So are there any
questions about all that?

01:01:33.400 --> 01:01:35.610
OK, cool.

